// SDL3, declared verbatim.
//
// **Everything that is C lives in this directory** — this one file of declarations over the library
// the machine has. Nothing here is meant to be called by a program: every name and every convention
// is C's, so a window is a pointer, a failure is `false` with the reason somewhere else, and a
// rectangle is four numbers with no methods on them. The module above this one turns those into
// sysl and is what an application imports.
//
// The split keeps two jobs apart. This one has to be **faithful** — a signature that disagrees with
// the header links perfectly and corrupts the call at run time, and nothing in this arrangement
// checks one against the other, so each declaration below was read out of SDL3's headers rather
// than remembered. The other one has to be **pleasant**, which is a different question and would
// otherwise be answered in the same breath.
//
// **There is no shim, which is the interesting half.** SDL3 needs none of the three things `15 §7`
// lists as reachable from C and from nothing else: every handle is a pointer SDL allocates and SDL
// frees, so this side never needs a size; `SDL_Surface` is public and ABI-stable in SDL3, so its
// fields are read straight out of SDL's storage; and the constants come from `c const` below rather
// than from a wrapper function each. The whole of the surface bound here is reachable by declaring
// it.

module sh.sysl.sdl3.c
@link("SDL3")

@include("SDL3/SDL.h")

// ## The numbers, asked for rather than written down
//
// Each of these is what the C compiler computes for SDL's own name, out of the headers named above,
// on the target being built. `15 §7` calls a transcribed constant "correct on one machine" with
// "nothing checking it", and this is what it asks for instead.
//
// **It found two that were wrong.** `SDL_WINDOW_ALWAYS_ON_TOP` had been written down as `0x8000`,
// which is `SDL_WINDOW_MOUSE_RELATIVE_MODE` — so a window asking to stay above the others got the
// pointer warped and hidden instead. `SDL_EVENT_TEXT_EDITING` had been written down as `0x304`,
// which is `SDL_EVENT_KEYMAP_CHANGED` — so an IME's composition events simply never matched. Both
// compiled, linked and ran; neither was anything a sysl test could have noticed, because the number
// it would have compared against is the number that was wrong.
//
// **SDL's headers are not on the toolchain's default search path**, so a consumer passes
// `--include-path` beside the `--link-path` this package already needs. `package.hocon` declares
// that requirement, which is what turns a missing header into a sentence naming this package rather
// than a clang error naming a file nobody wrote.

